  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Widespread showers and thunderstorms this afternoon \nand evening.\n\nShortwave energy moving over the northeast will support a period of \nwidespread showers and thunderstorms this afternoon and evening. \nModerate instability to the tune of 750-1000 J/kg of MUPCAPE and \nhigh PWATs near 2\" will support isolated to scattered areas of \ntorrential rainfall with embedded thunder and widespread\nmoderate showers elsewhere. There is a low-risk for some storms\nto be strong to severe with potentially damaging wind gusts. \nRelatively strong wind fields by July standards should mitigate \nthe overall flooding threat with mid-level flow around 30 \nknots supporting faster moving storms. However, we'll be on the\nlooking for any training areas of torrential rain that could \nlead to flooding in urban or poor drainage areas. Expect the \ngreatest shower and thunderstorm activity across eastern MA and \nRI between roughly 5PM and 1AM. Shower and thunderstorm \nactivity should wane overnight as stronger forcing shifts east \nof southern New England.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Another round of showers and thunderstorms \npossible Wednesday afternoon\n\nShowers and thunderstorms return tomorrow afternoon with instability \nincreasing in response to increased moisture. Dewpoints in the low \nto mid 70s may support SBCAPE values up to 1500 J/kg. Instability \nwill depend greatly on how much heating we get early tomorrow. The \namount of heating we get early will depend on how much clearing \noccurs between tonight and tomorrow morning. 12Z HREF guidance \nsuggests enough heating to support showers and thunderstorms across \neastern CT, MA, and RI during the mid-afternoon hours between 4 and \n8PM. Shear will be modest with roughly 20-25 knots in the 0-6km \nlayer. Overall severe threat is low, but a few isolated strong \nto severe storms will be possible as a cold front pushes through\nthe region. A cooler/drier air mass settles over the region \nWednesday night into Thursday with dewpoints falling to the \nupper 50s/low 60s. This should support a more comfortable day on\nThursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Seasonable temperatures and comfortable \nhumidity returns with dry conditions for the second half of the \nweek.\n\nAs the cold front finally pushes offshore Thursday, the upper level \ntrough over Quebec also pushes northeast into the Labrador Sea and a \nhigh pressure system begins to build over the Great Lakes. A drier \nairmass will be ushered into southern New England Thursday in the \nwake of the cold front, with dewpoints dropping into the mid to \nupper 50s, leading to much more comfortable feeling temperatures. \n925mb and 850mb temperatures drop to near 16C and 10C, respectively, \nfor the second half of the week, but intense sunlight and mostly \nclear skies will keep temperatures seasonable... roughly 80-85 \nthrough at least Sunday. Rain chances don't look to make a return \nuntil later Sunday evening, possible Sunday night into Monday, as \nsouth-southwesterly flow becomes more dominant and dewpoints begin \nto increase.",
